       wwggeettcchh gathers a key stroke from the terminal keyboard associated  with
       a  _c_u_r_s_e_s window _w_i_n.  nnccuurrsseess(3X) describes the variants of this func‐
       tion.

       When input is pending, wwggeettcchh returns an integer  identifying  the  key
       stroke;  for  alphanumeric and punctuation keys, this value corresponds
       to the character encoding used by the terminal.  Use of the control key
       as  a modifier often results in a distinct code.  The behavior of other
       keys depends on whether _w_i_n is in keypad mode; see  subsection  “Keypad
       Mode” below.

       If  no input is pending, then if the no-delay flag is set in the window
       (see nnooddeellaayy(3X)), the function returns EERRRR;  otherwise,  _c_u_r_s_e_s  waits
       until the terminal has input.  If ccbbrreeaakk(3X) has been called, this hap‐
       pens after one character is read.  If nnooccbbrreeaakk(3X) has been called,  it
       occurs  when  the  next  newline  is  read.   If hhaallffddeellaayy(3X) has been
       called, _c_u_r_s_e_s waits until a character is typed or the specified  delay
       elapses.

       If eecchhoo(3X) has been called, and the window is not a pad, _c_u_r_s_e_s writes
       the returned character _c to the window (at the cursor position) per the
